<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>ablation study</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the effect of hyperparameters on certification results.
Table  <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the hyperparameters and their values used in the experiments.
We conduct ablation studies on a randomly selected
scenario from the dataset on <em id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Graph Path (harmful target constraint)</em> distribution. Unless otherwise stated, we certify Llama-3.3-70b-instruct.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Default hyperparameters used in experiments.</figcaption>
<table id="A3.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A3.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Description</span></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A3.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A3.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the confidence interval for certification</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><math id="A3.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">num_samples</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Number of samples for certification</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right"><math id="A3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A3.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Lower threshold of embedding similarity to connect edges</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right"><math id="A3.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A3.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Higher threshold of embedding similarity to connect edges</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right"><math id="A3.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A3.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Weight assigned to high-weight neighbor set in <em id="A3.T3.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">AwR</em> distributions</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right">1</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A3.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Weight assigned to high-weight neighbor set in <em id="A3.T3.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">AwR</em> distributions</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right">2.5</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">qlen</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Length of the query sequence</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right">5</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">jailbreak_prob</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Probability of inserting jailbreak prompt before a query</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right"><math id="A3.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T3.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T3.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">setsize</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">Size of Query Set</td>
<td id="A3.T3.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_b">100</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Number of Samples</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess how our certification bounds change with the number of samples <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
we report bounds in Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.6 Size of Query Set ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>(a).
The ranges between lower and upper bounds shrink as <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases from small values, and stablize once <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\approx 50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\approx 50</annotation></semantics></math>.
In our main experiments, we therefore adopt <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=50</annotation></semantics></math> as a trade off between computational cost and statistical precision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Ratio of Weight</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the <em id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Adaptive with Rejection</em> distribution, <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weight assigned to the high-weight neighbor set, while <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the weight assigned to the low-weight neighbor set. Since the distribution is normalized after applying these weights (see Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" title="State space. ‣ 3.2 Graph Distributions from Markov Process ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), only the ratio <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}/\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}/\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the effective sampling probabilities, rather than their absolute values.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To study the influence of this ratio, we perform an ablation experiment by varying <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}/\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}/\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> across the values <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we require <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}&gt;\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}&gt;\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the minimum ratio considered is <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math>. We then evaluate the resulting certified bounds under these different settings. As shown in Figure <a href="#A3.F8.sf2" title="In Figure 8 ‣ C.6 Size of Query Set ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a>, both the lower and upper bounds generally increase as the ratio grows, suggesting that placing relatively more weight on the high-weight neighbor set improves the effectiveness of this distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Graph Thresholds</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph-based specifications rely on two thresholds, <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which determine
the sparsity of the similarity graph by controlling which edges are created based on embedding similarity.
To study their influence, we examine two settings:
(i) fixing <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{th}}=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{th}}=0.4</annotation></semantics></math> while varying <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{th}}\in\{0.7,0.8,0.9,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{th}}\in\{0.7,0.8,0.9,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(ii) fixing <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\text{th}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\text{th}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> while varying <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{th}}\in\{0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{th}}\in\{0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C.3 Graph Thresholds ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that the bounds do not change significantly for different thresholds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/thresh_low_fixed.svg" id="A3.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/thresh_high_fixed.svg" id="A3.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Ablation studies for certification bounds under different graph-threshold settings.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Length of Query Sequence</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F8.sf3" title="In Figure 8 ‣ C.6 Size of Query Set ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(c)</span></a> shows how certification bounds vary with the length of query sequence.
Increasing the length consistently shifts the bounds upward, suggesting that longer query sequences have higher probability of trigger catastrophic outcomes on models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Jailbreak Probability</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Certification bounds on <em id="A3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Random Node with Jailbreak</em> distribution is controlled by the jailbreak probability hyperparameter.
We study three LLMs: Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct, Deepseek-R1, and Claude-Sonnet-4 and show results in Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ C.5 Jailbreak Probability ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
We find that the effect of jailbreak probability is model-dependent:
for Llama-3.3-70B-instruct, bounds decrease slightly when the probability is <math id="A3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>,
but increase at lower values, suggesting that overly frequent jailbreak attempts can be detected.
For Deepseek-R1, which is less robust, bounds grow steadily as the probability increases.
For Claude-Sonnet-4, the bounds remain largely unchanged with values close to 0, indicating that it is defensive to jailbreak attempts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/llama_jailbreak_scenario20.svg" id="A3.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/126;" width="176" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/deepseek_jailbreak_scenario20.svg" id="A3.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/126;" width="176" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/claude_jailbreak_scenario20.svg" id="A3.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:176/126;" width="176" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Ablation studies for jailbreak probability on certification bounds for three LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.6 </span>Size of Query Set</h3>

<div id="A3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show the effect of the size of the query set we build specifications on.
Figure <a href="#A3.F8.sf4" title="In Figure 8 ‣ C.6 Size of Query Set ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(d)</span></a> shows that increasing the set size, i.e., the queries contained in the set, has little impact on the bounds, indicating that our results can generalize well to larger datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/nsamples_target.svg" id="A3.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/weights.svg" id="A3.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/qlen_scenario_20.svg" id="A3.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F8.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/qsetsize_scenario_20.svg" id="A3.F8.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((d)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A3.F8.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03969v1/variance_ci_boxplot.svg" id="A3.F8.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/176;" width="264" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((e)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Ablation studies for (a) number of samples,
(b) ratio of weights, (c)length of query sequence, (d) size of the query set, (e) variance.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.7 </span>Variance</h3>

<div id="A3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show the variance of our certification bounds in Figure <a href="#A3.F8.sf5" title="In Figure 8 ‣ C.6 Size of Query Set ‣ Appendix C ablation study ‣ Quantifying Risks in Multi-turn Conversation with Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(e)</span></a>, where we run the same experiment on one specification 10 times. We report the median and interquartile range (IQR) of the resulting 95% confidence lower and upper bounds. The results demonstrate that the variance is low, demonstrating the reliability of our certification procedure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>LLM Usage</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs were used in this work solely as general-purpose assistive tools to aid in polishing the writing and improving clarity of exposition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
